<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thibaw Min</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thibaw</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thibaw Min was the last king of the Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, reigning from 1878 to 1885. His reign began with the killing of many royal relatives and faced revolts among the Shan states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbin-confederacy">Limbin Confederacy</a>. A dispute over a British teak company led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-burmese-war">Third Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1885, after which he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thibaw-min.jpg" alt="Thibaw Min" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thibaw_Min.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thibaw was born Maung Yay Set, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> and one of his consorts, Laungshe Mibaya, whom Mindon banished from the palace court. As a boy he studied Buddhist texts at a monastery to win his father's favour and passed the Pahtamabyan religious examinations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyumashin">Hsinbyumashin</a>, the Queen of the Middle Palace, helped arrange his marriage to her eldest daughter Supayagyi, his half-sister by blood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Thibaw succeeded his father in 1878 after a bloody massacre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyumashin">Hsinbyumashin</a>, who had grown dominant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> in Mindon's last days, had royal relatives of close age who might claim the throne killed by edict, to make sure that Thibaw and her daughter would reign. At the coronation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supayalat">Supayalat</a> pushed in beside her sister to be anointed queen as well, so two queens were anointed together, which had never happened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. The treasury was low, taxes on peasants rose, and a national lottery, begun in 1878 on trial, ruined many families and was ended in 1880. In 1878 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hluttaw">Hluttaw</a> was enlarged from four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> to fourteen, and the kingdom was divided into ten districts under district ministers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, half the kingdom's former territory, had been under British occupation for thirty years, and the king was believed to intend to regain it. Relations worsened when he seemed to be aligning more closely with the French, and again in the incident called the Great Shoe Question, when British dignitaries refused to remove their shoes in the palace and were banished. A proclamation of 1885 calling on his countrymen to conquer Lower Burma gave the British a pretext to call him a tyrant who broke treaties.</p><p>An invasion force of 11,000 men under General Harry Prendergast reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> with little opposition, and the Burmese forces were defeated on 29 November 1885. The king and his family were taken to a steamer on the Irrawaddy in front of a large crowd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The British sent Thibaw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supayalat">Supayalat</a> and their two infant daughters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. They lived for twenty-four years at Outram Hall in Dharangaon, and in 1906 moved into a new residence built for them by the government, known as Thibaw's Palace. His allowance was raised that year to 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. He was reported to be reclusive but sponsored local festivals, particularly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>. He died in December 1916 aged 57 and was buried at Ratnagiri. The surviving royal family was relocated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1919.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thibaw Min” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Thibaw</b> (r. 1878–1885) was placed on the throne by a palace faction led by the queen Hsinbyumashin and her daughter Supayalat, and the execution of dozens of royal relatives in 1879 damaged his reputation in Britain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hsipaw, Shan <b>Thibaw</b>, was founded as a Shan principality in the Dokhtawady valley of the northern Shan plateau; tradition dates its ruling house to the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hsipaw-state">Hsipaw State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1880s the Mongnai saopha joined the Limbin Confederacy against <b>King Thibaw</b> and then the British, but submitted in 1887.</blockquote><a href="/state/mongnai-state">Mongnai State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.98,96.08&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Mandalay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7126 1352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thibaw Min"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7576 1622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7576 1622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.98,96.08&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
